{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS3XnaRnVzPaAqwtNuif","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed it: a `save_fact` now writes only the new fact to the cache (about 15 tokens) instead of rewriting the whole ~1000-token prompt. I tested this offline only, with no real API calls.\n\n**Cause:** the system prompt was one cached block, and the athlete's facts list sat inside it. Caching only reuses an exactly matching prefix, so every new fact changed the block and forced a full rewrite. That happened twice per save: once in the next round of the same message (the loop rebuilds the prompt each round), and again on the following message. This matches your log, where the call after each save shows `cache_read 0` and the write grows by about 15 tokens each time.\n\n**Fix (`build_system` in `bot.py`):** the system prompt is now split into blocks:\n1. The instructions, today's date and the profile (name, sport, zones, goal, language), with a cache marker.\n2. One block per fact, only ever appended to, with a cache marker on the last one.\n\nBecause saving a fact only adds a block at the end, the next request still starts with what was cached before. The API finds that earlier entry and reuses it. The model sees the same text as before; only the language line moved up into the profile and the facts now come last.\n\n**Decisions you might question:**\n- **The date stays in the stable block.** Putting it after the facts would break the \"only append\" layout. It also can't go in its own cached block: the cache needs at least 1024 tokens per cached prefix, and the tools plus instructions come to only about 970. The cost is one full rewrite per day, and the first message of a day is almost always uncached anyway because the cache expires after 5 minutes.\n- **I didn't touch the keyword tool filtering**, as NOTES.md asks. Tools come before the system prompt in what gets cached, though. A message that adds the planning or race tools still pays one full write (the 1305 and 1334 entries in your log). That's a separate cost from the memory problem.\n\n**Testing:** I added `tests/test_prompt_cache.py`, which uses a fake model that follows the caching rules and runs four messages, including two `save_fact`s. It passes with the new layout. Against the original code it fails with the same symptom as your log. Once this is deployed, `logs/usage.jsonl` should show each call after a save reading the full previous prompt from cache and writing about 15 tokens.\n\nI also updated the caching paragraph in the README. The full explanation is in `ANSWER.md`. Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1882,"cache_read_input_tokens":37867,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1882},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3b6e0e3-f798-4342-961d-3ea08af63fbf","uuid":"8f6a4e57-e817-4994-8a9c-4546c8c5b41c","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:38:04.545Z","request_id":"req_011CgS3XnG56C7L7CwJC1sgN"}
